ps://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/octet-stream","state":"uploaded","size":5162,"digest":"sha256:7165e67e8ce420b9fd69b7a393341fcd762c73c69775b7f8aefce345042197e8","download_count":4333,"created_at":"2026-10-26T10:41:09Z","updated_at":"2026-10-26T10:41:09Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/download/v1.28.0/mbx.zsh"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/assets/677839905","id":677839905,"node_id":"RA_kwDOT-nLJc4oZwQh","name":"packslip.sigstore.json","label":"","uploader":{"login":"github-actions[bot]","id":41898282,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/json","state":"uploaded","size":20793,"digest":"sha256:4534ee573e09b2caedd467e66297446276e7f4bf97af9f0ca51a1431edcf9730","download_count":7085,"created_at":"2026-10-26T10:41:17Z","updated_at":"2026-10-26T10:41:17Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/download/v1.28.0/packslip.sigstore.json"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/assets/677839157","id":677839157,"node_id":"RA_kwDOT-nLJc4oZwE1","name":"SHA256SUMS","label":"","uploader":{"login":"github-actions[bot]","id":41898282,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/octet-stream","state":"uploaded","size":705,"digest":"sha256:7b557c460f81b91a8be2ed9139d535c1d109b811c56d27cd5a0621b0ed162bef","download_count":122,"created_at":"2026-10-26T10:40:57Z","updated_at":"2026-10-26T10:40:57Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/download/v1.28.0/SHA256SUMS"}],"tarball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/tarball/v1.28.0","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/zipball/v1.28.0","body":"`mbx gc` can now remove cached results for Rust compiler versions you no longer use, and registry and Git `cargo install` builds now use the cache. This release also fixes target-directory size accounting, incremental state lost on build-script environment changes, toolchain mix-ups under an inherited `CARGO`, and several races between concurrent mbx processes. Plain `cargo` commands can also be sent through blaze as an experimental option.\n\n## Added\n\n- **Collect cached results for compiler versions you no longer use** ([#827](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/827), @jdx). After a Rust upgrade, the old compiler's results used to stay in the store until the size budget evicted them. The new opt-in setting `gc.rustc_max_age` lets automatic sweeps and `mbx gc` remove every cached rustc result for a compiler version (the exact `rustc -vV` identity, so two nightlies count separately) that has not been used within the window. Objects that a kept version also uses stay in the store. The most recently used version is always kept, and results that are not rustc compilations are left alone. The default is `none` (off).\n  ```sh\n  mbx settings set gc.rustc_max_age 90d   # or MBX_GC_RUSTC_MAX_AGE=90d\n  mbx gc --dry-run                        # preview what would be removed\n  ```\n  Use time is the result file's access time, or its modification time if the filesystem doesn't record access times. On `noatime` mounts, pick a window longer than the gap between rebuilds of a project.\n- **Experimental: send plain `cargo` builds through blaze** ([#832](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/832), @jdx). With `MBX_BLAZE=1` (or `blaze = true` in the config), the Cargo shim from `mbx setup` hands dev `build`, `run`, `test` and `check` commands to `ra-jit cargo`. That lets editors, scripts and agents that type `cargo build` use [blaze](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze)'s fast rebuilds. When blaze needs a real Cargo build, that build runs through mbx, so dependencies still restore from the cache. The setting is off by default and needs `ra-jit` on `PATH`. It only works on Linux and on macOS with Apple silicon. `--release`/`--profile` builds, `+toolchain` invocations, commands with flags before the subcommand, and other subcommands still run plain Cargo under mbx. blaze builds with its own Rust version, so its cache entries serve other blaze builds but not builds on your project's toolchain.\n- **`cargo install` from a registry or Git now uses the cache** ([#804](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/804), @jdx). Before, only `cargo install --path` was wrapped. Now `cargo install <crate>` and `cargo install --git <url>` run in an mbx session, through either the Cargo shim or `mbx install`, so a repeat install restores its compilations from the cache. Unless you pass `--target-dir` or set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, mbx builds in a scratch target directory and deletes it afterwards, overriding any `build.target-dir` in your Cargo config for that command. `cargo install --list` still goes straight to Cargo.\n\n## Fixed\n\n- **Target directory sizes count hard-linked files once** ([#793](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/793), @ViRb3). `mbx cache stats` and the `target.max_size` budget counted a file once for each target directory that hard-linked it. As a result, budget sweeps evicted more than needed and `mbx gc` reported freeing space that was still on disk. Each file now counts once, and gc weighs each target directory by the space that deleting it would actually free. `mbx gc --json` adds `targets.released_bytes`, and the text report shows the released amount when it differs from the logical one. When free space is below `gc.min_free_size`, gc now keeps cleaning as long as its last round deleted something.\n- **A changed build-script environment value no longer throws away incremental state** ([#830](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/830), @jdx, @ViRb3). If a crate read a `cargo:rustc-env` value through `env!`/`option_env!`, such as a Git hash or version, it compiled from scratch every time that value changed and printed `incremental state was not reused: compiler environment input changed`. mbx now treats the change like a source edit: the crate keeps its private state and the warning is gone. mbx first checks the shared cache for the new value, so after pulling a commit that CI already built, the crate and its dependents can restore CI's results.\n- **Dependencies hit the same cache entries whether output goes to a terminal or a pipe** ([#801](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/801), @jdx). Cargo passes `--diagnostic-width` only when stderr is a terminal, so builds from a terminal, a pipe (agents, editors, scripts), or a terminal of a different width missed each other's dependency entries. For registry and Git dependencies, which build with `--cap-lints=allow`, the width is no longer part of the cache key. Workspace crates still include it.\n- **Builds started with an inherited `CARGO` use one toolchain** ([#808](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/808), @jbellis). A Cargo subcommand that set `CARGO` but dropped `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN` could build registry crates with the default toolchain and workspace crates with the pinned one, which failed with `E0514 ... compiled by an incompatible version of rustc`. When `CARGO` points to a Cargo inside a rustup toolchain, mbx now uses that toolchain's `rustc`. An explicit `RUSTC`, `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN`, `+toolchain`, or `build.rustc` still takes precedence.\n- **Workspace wrappers added by Cargo subcommands are respected** ([#819](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/819), @jdalton, @jdx). When a subcommand such as ra-jit adds a `RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER` after the mbx session starts, mbx now leaves workspace crates to that wrapper and keeps their existing `.rcgu.o` files instead of deleting them. Dependencies are still cached. The build summary lists these crates as `workspace-wrapper` bypasses ([#832](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/832)).\n- **Restores no longer keep warning about missing blobs after gc** ([#825](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/825), @jdx). After a sweep under size or disk pressure, results whose output tree had lost a file were kept. Each later restore printed `prediction was not restored: cached action is missing blob ...` and recompiled, sometimes for hours. Sweeps that evict anything now drop those results, and `mbx cache verify` reports them.\n- **Fewer warnings that need no action** ([#826](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/826), @jdx). Four warnings now log at debug level in the expected cases: build scripts that probe `$RUSTC` before Cargo has written their declarations, C compiler detection probes with no `-o`, predictions that name an input that doesn't exist in this checkout, and two checkouts publishing the same key at once when the existing result is intact. They still appear in other cases.\n- **Automatic gc on sandboxed hosts** ([#807](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/807), @jbellis). A background sweep killed by a sandbox when the build exited, such as Codex's `bwrap --unshare-pid`, still counted as done, so most automatic sweeps on such hosts never ran. A sweep is now recorded only after it finishes, so the next build retries. `gc.interval` now counts from the end of the last finished sweep.\n- **Concurrent mbx processes no longer undo each other's cache and target work** ([#809](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/809), [#811](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/811), @jbellis). Two processes repairing the same damaged action record could overwrite each other, and collection could delete a record that another process had just repaired. Both now lock the record and check it again before acting. `mbx cache import` and `mbx adopt` now reserve the target directory. If another command, such as `mbx clean` or gc, is using the target, they stop with `another command is using the target directory for ...; retry when it finishes` instead of racing it. Replacing an outdated managed target keeps the old target reserved until it has been renamed.\n- **Ctrl+C on Windows no longer leaves session directories behind** ([#824](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/824), @jdx). Each cancelled `mbx exec` or `mbx cargo` build used to leave an `%TEMP%\\mbx-session-*` directory. mbx now exits normally with the child's exit code and removes the directory. Directories left by earlier versions are not removed.\n- **Remote task manifests stay under the reader's size limit** ([#798](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/798), @jdx). A task action manifest could grow past the 16 MiB limit that remote readers enforce. After that, every lookup and upload for that task failed. Manifests are now trimmed, oldest predictions first, to fit 15 MiB. Manifests already oversized on a remote stay unreadable until they are removed.\n\n## Performance\n\n- **Faster build-script `OUT_DIR` restores** ([#831](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/831), @jdx). With `build_script_execution` on, restoring an `OUT_DIR` now looks up files in batches and copies them in parallel, and the cache agent checks new blobs concurrently. For aws-lc-sys, the restore dropped from 1.91 s to 0.45 s on Windows with Defender real-time scanning, and from 100 ms to 35 ms on Linux.\n- **Deleting a checkout's incremental state no longer blocks other builds** ([#810](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/810), @jbellis). `mbx clean` and gc used to hold the incremental cache lock for the whole recursive delete, which took up to 9 seconds on large checkouts. They now rename the tree, release the lock, and then delete it. If the process dies before deleting, the next collection removes the leftover.\n\n## Upgrade notes\n\n- The per-checkout record of which crates are being edited moves to version 2 ([#830](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/830)). On the first build after upgrading, a workspace crate that was using private incremental state compiles once without it, then switches back.\n\n## New Contributors\n\n* @jdalton made their first contribution in [#819](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/819)\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/v1.27.0...v1.28.0","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/424106281/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"mentions_count":4}